  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each of the given deadlines, a solution to at least one Millennium Prize Problem (excluding Poincar\u00e9) will have been publicly available, with AI contributing a new mathematical argument or proof step. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050. Let me break this down.\n\nFirst, the Millennium Prize Problems are seven mathematical problems that were unsolved in 2000, each with a $1 million prize. Poincar\u00e9 was solved in 2003, so the remaining six are the focus. The event requires a correct and complete solution, publicly available, with AI contributing a new mathematical step.\n\nI need to consider the timeline for math problems being solved. These are notoriously hard. For example, Fermat's Last Theorem took over 350 years. But these problems fell in 2000, so maybe some are closer to being solved? Let me recall that as of 2023:\n\n- Navier-Stokes: Still open. Some progress but no complete solution.\n- Riemann Hypothesis: Ongoing work, but no proof.\n- P vs NP: Still open.\n- Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer: Open.\n- Hodge Conjecture: Open.\n- Yang-Mills: Open.\n\nSo all six are still unsolved. So the question is, with AI's current role in math, will AI contribute to solving one of these, and by when?\n\nAI in math has been used for proof assistants, exploring patterns, and even in some cases like the recent proof of 80-year-old problem. But these problems are extremely hard. For example, Tim Gowers' prize in 2021 (resolved by AI-inspired theorem) was for a specific problem that's not one of the Millenniums.\n\nThe key here is \"identifiable new mathematical argument or proof step\" by AI. That suggests AI isn't just verifying or assisting in calculations, but contributing a novel part of the proof.\n\nBut given the difficulty of these problems, even with AI, it's hard to say. Let me think about the current state of AI in mathematics. There's DeepMind's Euclid, which has shown promise, but nothing at the level of Millennium Problems yet.\n\nThen, for the deadlines. How quickly can AI advance to the point of contributing to such a solution? Let's assume that by 2030, maybe AI could assist in some aspects, but most of the proof would be human. For example, the solution of the Kepler conjecture involved rigorous computations, perhaps more feasible for AI in the future. But the Millennium problems are more abstract.\n\nPartial progress won't count here. So a complete solution is needed. Let me consider the time scale. If all six are unsolved for over 20 years (Poincar\u00e9 was solved in 2003, others have been around longer). So each of these has probably been attacked by many mathematicians over decades, without success.\n\nThe question is whether AI can contribute a key insight. Maybe AI can explore vast mathematical landscapes, generate conjectures, or automate parts of a proof. For example, if the solution involves very complex certain cases where AI develops a new theorem or lemma. Or perhaps automated reasoning systems can help fill in gaps.\n\nBut even given that, the probability is low. By 2026, just a year and a half from now? Unlikely. By 2030, maybe 5%? By 2035? Maybe 10-15%? By 2040, maybe 20-30%? By 2050, maybe 40%? But I need to be cautious.\n\nAlso, the problem requires the solution to be \"publicly available,\" meaning the proof has to be made public, and the AI's contribution must be identifiable. So even if a solution is made in a secretive way, it has to be public. But given the academic environment, probably the work would be published, and if AI contributed, that would be acknowledged.\n\nProbability should be non-decreasing with each deadline. Let's consider:\n\nFrom 2026 onwards:\n\n2026: 5%? But maybe even lower. Let's say 0.02 (2%) by 2026.\n\n2030: Maybe 0.05 (5%).\n\n2035: Maybe 0.1 (10%).\n\n2040: Maybe 0.2 (20%).\n\n2050: Maybe 0.5 (50%).\n\nBut wait, these are estimates. Another angle: the difficulty of the problems. Let's take P vs NP as an example. Many researchers think it's extremely hard. If AI can assist in proving some lower bounds, which is needed for P vs NP, perhaps. But even so, decades of research by top humans haven't solved it. Maybe AI plus humans can solve it in 30 years, but by 2050 that's possible?\n\nBy 2050, if AI is fully integrated into mathematical research, maybe AI is assisting in developing the arguments, even if humans are the ones who formulated the strategy. So by 2050, maybe 50-70% chance.\n\nBut since the question allows for any one of the six, and not all require the same level of technical difficulty. For example, the Riemann Hypothesis might be more approachable with advanced analysis using AI's ability to compute and check vast amounts of data or patterns.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Nakai-Yoshida Local-Global Principle (which is a Millennium problem?) Wait, the six are:\n\n1. Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer (BSD) conjecture: about elliptic curves and their rational points.\n\n2. Hodge conjecture: algebraic geometry, cohomology classes.\n\n3. Navier-Stokes: existence and smoothness of solutions.\n\n4. P vs NP: computational complexity.\n\n5. Riemann Hypothesis: zeros of zeta function.\n\n6. Yang-Mills existence and mass gap: quantum field theory.\n\nEach of these has different nature. For example, Navier-Stokes is about PDEs, which might be solvable via concrete analysis, while others are more abstract.\n\nAI's role in each varies. For example, in Navier-Stokes, maybe numerical simulations with AI could lead to insights. For the Riemann Hypothesis, pattern recognition in L-functions. For P vs NP, maybe automated theorem proving could find a counterexample or a lower bound.\n\nBut again, these are all very difficult problems. I think the probabilities should increase slowly. Let me adjust my initial estimates.\n\nGiven that, let me structure:\n\n- 2026: 0.02 (2%)\n\n- 2030: 0.03 (3%)\n\n- 2035: 0.05 (5%)\n\n- 2040: 0.1 (10%)\n\n- 2050: 0.2 (20%)\n\nAlternatively, some might argue that as AI capabilities increase, the probability should increase more rapidly. But maybe the probability of AI contributing to a solution is low unless the problem is tractable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the probability of AI helping before 2040 is 5%, and by 2050 up 20-30%.\n\nBut given the difficulty and that these problems are open for long time, maybe the probability is even lower. For example, maybe 1% by 2030, 5% by 2050.\n\nBut given the requirement for an AI contribution, maybe higher.\n\nAlternatively, considering that some problems might be solved by human mathematicians without AI, but the question requires AI contribution. So maybe the probability is lower.\n\nBut the event is that AI contributed to the solution. So if a problem is solved by humans, but AI made a key step in it, that counts. If it's a human-only solution, it doesn't count.\n\nSo perhaps AI's involvement is another hurdle. Thus, the probability is even lower than just the probability of solving the problem.\n\nTherefore, I might adjust probabilities downward.\n\nSo for example:\n\n2026: 0.01 (1%)\n\n2030: 0.02 (2%)\n\n2035: 0.05 (5%)\n\n2040: 0.1 (10%)\n\n2050: 0.2 (20%)\n\nBut the problem is that, with the current capabilities, I don't see a clear path to any of these problems being solved with AI's help soon. Maybe by 2050, AI will be more integrated into mathematical discovery, so maybe the probability is higher. But 30 years is a long time, and progress in mathematics is unpredictable.\n\nAnother angle: Evolution of AI capabilities. We are in the early stages of AI in mathematics. As of now, computerized theorem proving is still in early stages. Systems like Lean can formalize proofs, but don't solve open problems. So for AI to contribute to a solution of a Millennium problem requires significant progress in AI's ability to think mathematically.\n\nPerhaps in 5 years (by 2030), AI can assist in more complex proofs, but not at the level of solving a Millennium problem. In 2035, with more time, maybe 1-5% chance. By 2040, perhaps 10-15%, and by 2050, up to 20-30%. These are rough estimates.\n\nAlso, the answer must be a single JSON object with the keys and probabilities, non-decreasing with the deadline. So the probabilities must increase or stay the same over time.\n\nTherefore, making my final estimates as:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.015\n\n2035: 0.02\n\n2040: 0.03\n\n2050: 0.05\n\nBut wait, that's not accelerating much. Maybe adjust: higher increase over time.\n\nFor example:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.02\n\n2035: 0.05\n\n2040: 0.1\n\n2050: 0.3\n\nThat seems plausible. By 2050, maybe 30% chance.\n\nBut I need to be cautious. Another thought: maybe the probability remains low and barely increasing. So for example:\n\n2026: 0.005\n\n2030: 0.008\n\n2035: 0.01\n\n2040: 0.02\n\n2050: 0.05\n\nAlternatively, given that the forecast date is mid-2025, and the deadlines are up to 2050, so 25 years at most from forecast date.\n\nBut how much time has passed since 2025?\n\nAs of now, mid-2025, up to 2050 is 25 years. The timeline for AI to reach that level of ability is unknown. If AI development is progressing rapidly, maybe higher chances, but how?\n\nWell, I have to create my best estimates given existing knowledge.\n\nSince the probabilities need to be non-decreasing, I'll structure them as follows:\n\n- 2026: 1% (since it's only 1 year or 16 months away, from mid-2025 to Dec 2026)\n\n- 2030: 3% (5-year gap)\n\n- 2035: 5% (10-year gap)\n\n- 2040: 10% (15 years)\n\n- 2050: 20% (25 years)\n\nThat seems a reasonable progression, with probabilities increasing as the deadlines advance, acknowledging the longer time for progress.\n\nSo the JSON would be:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.03,\n  \"2035\": 0.05,\n  \"2040\": 0.1,\n  \"2050\": 0.2\n}\n\nThis meets the criteria: probabilities are numbers between 0 and 1, non-decreasing with time. Each step increases, reflecting the longer time for AI advancements to bridge the gap towards a solution with AI contribution.\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.03,\n  \"2035\": 0.05,\n  \"2040\": 0.1,\n  \"2050\": 0.2\n}",
